s to that  infection determine if you have an  infection because again you can image  these iron oxide nanoparticles and then  in real time you can watch is that  infection  reducing is it ineffective your  treatment and you need another injection  of iron oxide nanoparticles you can  watch the treatment occur while you're  in the hospital or in that doctors suite  here's a quick experiment to show you  how well a magnet does in terms of these  iron oxide nanoparticles these two  images are the same field of view one is  a bright field image where you can see  the dark black area is a magnet  so we put a magnet underneath the petri  dish we add all these bacteria grow a  biofilm and you can see you know in the  bright field image where that magnet is  situated again the same image just under  florescent mode is on the right and this  is what's called a live dead stain so we  apply our magnetic nanoparticles and  then we stain four green means that the  bacteria are living red means the  bacteria are dead so you can see right  where we place the magnet that's drawing  the iron oxide nanoparticles into the  biofilm into the bacteria and it's  killing them where we don't have the  magnet we're still seeing live bacteria  so again proof of principle that you can  use a magnet to drive these  nanoparticles inside a biofilm to kill  bacteria  another chemistry that is getting a lot  of attention today is called selenium  selenium is part of our diet you find it  in many foods in fact where this  interest in selenium came from is there  have been studies to correlate if you do  not have the daily recommended  nutritional value of selenium in your  diet you're more likely to get cancer  then if you have the nutritional diet of  selenium so there's a thought that  selenium might be involved in  anti-cancer properties in fact there are  now containers of selenium vitamins of  selenium that you can purchase well  there's many similarities between  killing cancer and killing bacteria so  we've been very interested in taking  that wonderful information on how  selenium is being used to kill cancer  cells kill tumors and kill bacteria and  just to make a long story short selenium  is often proposed to kill cancer cells  and bacteria as we have shown since it  interferes with bacteria that are  proteins that are inside bacteria so as  soon as selenium crosses the membrane  it dissolves the selenium ions interfere  with some proteins in bacteria and  disrupt their function which results in  death of the bacteria same thing for a  cancer cell the selenium gets inside the  cancer cell interferes with some  bacteria with some proteins that are in  cancer cells and that kills the cancer  cell so we were one of the first groups  to develop a chemical reaction that can  coat almost anything with selenium  nanoparticles so we're doing in the body  applications outside the body  applications bacteria are everywhere so  we're coding a lot of surfaces with  selenium this slide shows you how we  took an uncoated paper towel and B and  coated it with selenium nanoparticles so  all of those little white dots in a our  selenium nanoparticles it's a simple  chemical reaction we actually have a  spray we can spray computer screens door  knobs you name it we can spray this  selenium nanoparticles and they stick to  these surfaces so when doing so we're  seeing great decrease in bacterial  attachment decrease in bacterial growth  here's just some of the raw data where  this is for again Staphylococcus aureus  but we've seen this for many bacteria  where you see significantly reduced v  log reduction in bacterial growth after  24 hours 48 hours 72 hours when coding  these paper towels with selenium so it  might sound trivial but paper towels are  a source of bacterial contamination  think of a hospital in the hospital you  wash your hands after you know you're in  the restroom and you throw away the  paper towel while there is now bacteria  on that paper towel 