 assembled nanomaterials which is growing  and growing in popularity because it's  sort of like a pre-programmed nano  material you know it will self assemble  into a controllable way to kill bacteria  our version of self assembled materials  we're using to fight bacteria are called  antimicrobial peptides so what we do is  we simply take things like hydrophobic  amino acids that can attach to the  membrane of a bacteria we then add a  group that's hydrophobic add a group  that's hydrophilic so that it will self  assemble into a rod to then interject  into the bacterial membrane kind of stab  the bacterial membrane and cause the  inside to come out what is nice about  these self-assembled antimicrobial  peptides is they are very strong  interaction with a bacterial cell  membrane as you can see on the left but  they have a weak interaction with  mammalian cell membranes and really the  reason for that is mammalian cells which  we don't want to kill have things like  cholesterol they have other proteins  that are present in the cell wall which  won't be stabbed or won't be influenced  by these self-assembled chemistry's so  they're really selective towards killing  bacteria which is very very important so  here's a visual description of what  these these molecules do again we design  them to self-assemble into rods they  come in close contact with the bacterial  membrane that's pictured here and then  they start going into the bacterial  membrane once they go through part of it  goes through the bacterial membrane part  of it is inside the cell that generates  a whole and from that whole everything  from inside the bacteria comes out and  it dies so these structures the ability  of these self-assembled materials to  assemble into these fibers nanofibers is  very important  because you need something long enough  to go through the membrane and you need  something small enough that it doesn't  come out the other end but it goes right  into that that cell body a bacterial  body here's the specifics of a molecule  that one of the molecules of many that  we're studying and again we need a  hydrophobic tail group we need a beta  sheet forming domain a charge domain and  then a functional domain the hydrocarbon  tail group helps with self-assembly and  a fiber like formation the beta sheet  also helps in that self-assembly and the  functional domain is what allows us to  target ecoli or Staphylococcus aureus or  Pseudomonas aeruginosa so we can use  that red region to target a specific  bacteria so again the picture of how  this happens we create monomers of these  cationic self-assemble peptides they  self assemble into cylindrical  nanoparticles or rods they first attach  to the cell membrane but then because of  their charge they go in to the cell  creating a hole creating this leakage of  the cytoplasm which results in their  death but please keep in mind the same  thing does not happen for the mammalian  cells because of the inability of these  particles these fibers to get through a  mammalian cell membrane so again a  diagram of this specific chemistry we're  looking at if you're interested in that  the hydrocarbon group this beta forming  sheet and then the functional group  there in orange and and how it looks you  know if we diagram this in terms of the  chemical structure so what I want to  highlight is just a real quick study  that we did with these molecules in  which we we highlighted the reason why  they need to form nano structures so  nanotechnology is all about the  importance of the nano size scale so  what we have done is a series of studies  that show how important it is that these  assemble into a nano fiber as compared  to a micron fiber or a nano sphere the  Nano fiber is very very important so we  created our material step by step and we  added it to bacteria so the first step  was just this by card in peptide this  orange region as I mentioned before is  the peptide that targets the cell  membrane the bacterial cell membrane so  we use that as one material another  material we used we call CV kPa this is  really the beta sheet self-assembly part  so we added that just to the peptide  that was our second material our third  material is the whole entire thing so we  now have the hydrocarbon backbone the  beta sheet formation and that region  that attaches to the bacterial membrane  and as you'll see it's all three of  these together the AC a PA that works if  you remove any part of that this three  component system if you remove any part  the whole thing doesn't work and it  doesn't kill bacteria  we of course determined the critical  micelle concentration so this is the  critical concentration at which each of  these components would kill bacteria and  you can see just the by Cardon just the  peptide that attaches to the membrane  doesn't kill bacteria at all under the  critical micelle concentration  there's na not applicable it doesn't  kill it the self-assembly region with  the beta sheet formation and the by  Cardon and the hydrophobic backbone had  a critical micelle concentration of  around 50 which means that's the amount  necessary to kill the bacteria here's  what these structures look like again  you can see these nano fiber formations  of course if you increase the  concentration of these self-assembled  materials you'll get longer and longer  chains so we need to have the right  concentration that will penetrate that  bacterial wall we  confirmed secondary structure to show  that each one of these three materials  is the structure we think it is and most  importantly what I want to share with  you is the ability of these materials to  reduce bacterial growth so we looked at  gram positive bacteria MRSA you can see  with time we're able to inhibit  bacterial growth with gram negative  bacteria this is MDR ecoli or multi  drug-resistant ecoli we completely  eliminated using these self-assembled  molecules live dead stains similar to  what I described before green means  alive red means dead for MRSA the you  know horrible drug resistant  Staphylococcus aureus this  self-assembled molecule almost  completely inhibited MRSA growth multi  drug-resistant coli again you can see  high efficacy at killing those bacteria  but here's the part that I mentioned  before you need each part of this  self-assembled molecule to work so on  this slide are the building blocks by  themselves not the collective molecule  but just each building block the by card  in peptide the peptide AMPA phil they  don't work the y-axis here is the number  of colony forming units it's the same no  matter what concentration you use so  only when you have the combination of  the beta forming sheets the by card in  peptide and the hydrophobic backbone do  you get that fiber to then kill the  bacteria so that's a real definition of  nanotechnology has to form a nano  structure we've also looked at the  mechanism of action for those of you  that are interested in bacterial  lipopolysaccharides our major component  of the membrane of gram-negative  bacteria and you can see here the red is  our self-assembled molecule that is what  attaches the most to that LPS region of  the bacterial membrane if we just had  the building blocks which are in black  and blue we don't get a lot of  attachment  and this last slide before I summarize  is so important because it really shows  you what's happening to the bacteria so  the the nanorods that we created you can  see when we expose them to MRSA or  e.coli the membrane is getting disrupted  and the cell is basically internal  components are coming out of the  bacteria so it's clearly dying as soon  as we disrupt that cell membrane whereas  the controls you see the cell membrane  is intact so the I guess the very last  thing that I'll show here is  cytotoxicity so what important part is  we have identified a concentration at  which these self-assembled materials  work for bacteria but they do not work  and and they do not kill a human dermal  fibroblasts or healthy cells that we  don't want to kill so they are selective  towards bacteria and we can charge them  if you charge these self-assembled  materials they'll coat surfaces so tying  this back into the beginning of my  presentation you can actually use these  on medical devices coat them on medical  devices by inducing a charge and that  will help contact kill bacteria so I  hope I shared with you a couple of  future directions current directions in  nanotechnology I showed you some current  fda-approved processes that we call nano  texturing or nano structured surfaces  which are eliminating infection without  using antibiotics I also provided you a  couple of examples of nanoparticles a  little more risky a little more work  involved in determining bio distribution  and other things and I let I ended the  presentation with a really futuristic  direction of self-assembled  nanomaterials where you can now have a  chemistry that can poke and prod and  disrupt a bacterial membrane to cause it  to die all three of these areas are  really really exciting intersections  between nanotechnology and eliminating  bacterial growth without resorting  drugs but let me leave you with one  final thought and that final thought is  in all of these approaches most likely  you would still have to determine if  someone has bacteria in them and then  give them a solution I gave you a small  example of an immune cell sensor that  we're developing  here's another sensor that we're  developing and the purpose of these  sensors which I'm convinced will be the  future of medicine the promise of these  sensors is that they in fact can  determine if bacteria are present  surrounding your implant and then if so  kill them we don't have that technology  now we don't know in real time if this  is occurring after you get an implant so  quickly what we're doing is we're taking  a hip implant we're growing carbon  nanotubes that's what's pictured in the  bottom right off of the surface carbon  nanotubes can measure the resistance and  therefore determine if a bacteria is  attaching to the implant bacteria have a  different resistance than bone which is  different than scar tissue so we can  identify if bacteria are on your implant  in real time that information is then  sent to a computer or your iPhone to  tell you that you have bacteria  surrounding some of your implant we've  already made an appointment for you with  your doctor so it is an intelligent  system that communicates and talks to  you about what's happening inside your  body the third part to the sensor is  that we developed a polymer that can  release a nano particle or a  self-assembled material on demand and it  does so because it's a conductive  polymer that we apply an electromagnetic  field it will degrade and release  whatever's inside of it so we now have  the ability to determine is a bacteria  present communicate to a handheld device  to your clinician yes it is present and  then thirdly release something  nanoparticles self-assembled material to  kill the bacteria if it's present this  is a true intelligence  and material that has to be the future  of our research in medicine so I'd like  to thank you for paying attention here  are some acknowledgments for funding  sources of course that have occurred  throughout throughout my career which  has enabled us to to do this and of  course my lab group has enabled me to do  all this research I encourage you go to  our website learn more about the things  we're doing nanotechnology and nano  medicine is a fascinating area of  research that I hope more people get  involved in thank you
 

